anv: AV1 HuC BRC runs two PAK passes, as media-driver

media-driver gives every BRC frame two passes (EncodeAv1VdencFeatureManager::SetPassNum): the second BRC update
(UPD_PAKPassNum 1) sees the first pass's size and asks for a re-encode with HUC_STATUS bit 31, and the second PAK
pass runs only then (AddCondBBEndFor2ndPass). The pass is a second-level batch behind the conditional end, so
only it is skipped; its calls into the kernel's batches and the header variant batches nest one level deeper
(measured on Xe2: a second-level call from a second-level batch returns, and a conditional end there ends only
that level). The loop filter / CDEF variants of the re-encode are captured inside the pass, so the obu_size patch
after the passes matches the bitstream either way.
First test, Netflix Aerial 1080p60 VBR 3 Mbit/s, 600 frames: 1 B frame 3813 -> 3529 kbit/s (key frames 161 -> 122 kB),
2 B frames 3266 -> 3150 kbit/s; all frames decode with dav1d.

